  "worked_solution": "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the first branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(2)=-4-2=-6.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2.$ $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- Statement II is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(-4-x) \\\\  & =-4-2 \\\\  & =-6 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(-3x) \\\\  & =-3(2) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ exists:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-6\\,\\,\u2713\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have that ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-6=f(2).$  $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since all statements I-III are true, according to the continuity test, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
